---
description: Keep selfPos, cardFactories, and collection callbacks typed without any
alwaysApply: true
---

# Callback Typing Convention

Do not annotate callback parameters with `any` in these three zones:

- `selfPos` handlers on `cardFactory`, `tableFactory`, and `createTree`
- `cardFactories` handlers on `segFactory`
- `find` / `forEach` / `map` / `findIndex` callbacks on `EReadCollection` and extending interfaces

Using `any` here is bad practice — it disables inference from preprocessor-generated `GenSegments` / `GenCards` types. Prefer **implicit typing** (omit annotations) or **explicit correct types** from generated files.

A helper returning `: any` (for example `createSelfPos`) prevents strongly-typed `selfPos` inference on `cardFactory`. Annotating `place: any` rejects the typed place segment from `GenSegments`.

Avoid unnecessary `as unknown as` at factory boundaries when runtime and facade prop types are aligned. See [Avoid unnecessary type casts](../../README.md#avoid-unnecessary-type-casts).

See [README Callback Typing Convention](../../README.md#callback-typing-convention).

## Parameter naming

| Callback | 1st arg | 2nd arg |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `selfPos` | `place` — segment the card is positioned along | `self` — the card itself |
| `cardFactories` | `ortho` — orthogonal segment | `self` — the segment owning the factory |

Use `_` for unused parameters. When only one argument is used, name it by position (`place`, `ortho`, or `self`).

## selfPos

Keep handlers inline inside the `cardFactory` object. Do not extract them into helper functions returning `any`.

Incorrect:

```typescript
const createSelfPos = (element: ChemicalElement): any => ({
  PeriodListSeg: (place) =>
    place.nested.find(
      (periodSeg: any) => periodSeg.attrs.period === element.period
    ),
});

selfPos: createSelfPos(element)
```

Also bad: `PeriodListSeg: (place: any) => ...`

Correct:

```typescript
selfPos: {
  PeriodListSeg: (place) =>
    place.nested.find(
      (periodSeg) => periodSeg.attrs.period === element.period
    ),
}
```

## selfPos scope

List `selfPos` handlers for every coordinate (place segment type) the card may need. XorLAB calls only the handler whose key matches `place.typeName` along the card's actual placement path — extra keys are inert. A parent [Layer Anchor](../../README.md#layer-anchor-pattern) still scopes which axes apply at runtime. Do not branch on placement axis with conditional spreads.

Incorrect:

```typescript
selfPos: {
  WeekSeg: (place) => /* ... */,
  ...(slot.axis === "group"
    ? { GroupListSeg: (place) => /* ... */ }
    : { TeacherListSeg: (place) => /* ... */ }),
}
```

Correct — handlers for every coordinate:

```typescript
selfPos: {
  WeekSeg: (place) => /* ... */,
  WeekdaySeg: (place) => /* ... */,
  GroupListSeg: (place) => /* ... */,
  TeacherListSeg: (place) => /* ... */,
}
```

See [README selfPos scope — no axis conditionals](../../README.md#selfpos-scope--no-axis-conditionals).

## selfPos — no completeness guards

Do not gate `selfPos` on “attrs complete?” checks (e.g. `isCompleteGroupSlot`). For slot-selection display cards, nest the card only when a slot is selected with complete attrs; leave the list empty when cleared. If the card is nested, `selfPos` may trust attrs; if nothing is selected, the card is absent and `selfPos` never runs. Completeness checks inside `selfPos` paper over a lifecycle bug.

See [README SelectController](../../README.md#selectcontroller) (slot value) and [`examples/timetable/src/domain/SlotSelection.ts`](../../examples/timetable/src/domain/SlotSelection.ts).

## cardFactories

Keep handlers inline inside the `segFactory` object.

Incorrect:

```typescript
cardFactories: {
  LabelPlaceSeg: (_: any, self: any) =>
    cardFactory({ /* ... */ }),
}
```

Correct:

```typescript
cardFactories: {
  LabelPlaceSeg: (_, self) =>
    cardFactory({ /* ... */ }),
}
```

## ECollection callbacks

Incorrect:

```typescript
place.nested.find((item: any) => item.attrs.period === element.period)
```

Correct:

```typescript
place.nested.find((item) => item.attrs.period === element.period)
```
